    "topic": "the Oxford comma that cost 5 million dollars",
    "summary": "The serial (Oxford) comma comes before the conjunction in a list of three or more. Chicago recommends it and Oxford University Press uses it; the AP Stylebook and New York Times generally omit it. Maine's overtime law exempted 'canning, processing, preserving, freezing, drying, marketing, storing, packing for shipment or distribution of' certain foods. Oakhurst Dairy's delivery drivers argued that without a comma after 'shipment', 'distribution' was part of packing, so they were owed overtime. In March 2017 the First Circuit found the law ambiguous and sided with the drivers; Judge Barron opened: 'For want of a comma, we have this case.' In 2018 Oakhurst agreed to pay $5 million to settle, admitting no fault.",
    "angle": "One missing comma, 5 million dollars.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "The serial comma is a comma placed after the penultimate term in a list, just before the conjunction; it's called the Oxford comma after Oxford University Press, where it is house style.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_comma",
            "title": "Serial comma - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "a comma placed after the penultimate term in a list"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "The Chicago Manual of Style strongly recommends it; the AP Stylebook says not to put a comma before the conjunction in a simple series, and the New York Times generally omits it.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_comma",
            "title": "Serial comma - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "do not put a comma before the conjunction in a simple series"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "Maine's overtime exemption covered 'packing for shipment or distribution', with no comma after 'shipment'; Oakhurst Dairy's delivery drivers argued this was one activity, packing, which they don't do.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-ca1-16-01901/pdf/USCOURTS-ca1-16-01901-0.pdf",
            "title": "O'Connor v. Oakhurst Dairy, 1st Cir., March 13, 2017",
            "quote": "packing for shipment or distribution"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "On March 13, 2017, the First Circuit found the statute ambiguous and ruled for the drivers; the opinion opens 'For want of a comma, we have this case.'",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-ca1-16-01901/pdf/USCOURTS-ca1-16-01901-0.pdf",
            "title": "O'Connor v. Oakhurst Dairy, 1st Cir., March 13, 2017",
            "quote": "For want of a comma, we have this case"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "text": "In 2018, Oakhurst Dairy agreed to pay $5 million to settle the drivers' overtime claims, admitting no fault.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/litigation/oakhurst-dairys-5m-settlement-driven-by-grammar-rules",
            "title": "Oakhurst Dairy's $5M Settlement Driven by Grammar Rules - Bloomberg Law, 2018",
            "quote": "agrees to pay $5 million to settle drivers' overtime claims"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    ],
